  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1010 PM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA low pressure system currently over northern California will \nmove across the Great Basin and move across the central portions\nof our CWA, mainly impacting the central mountain corridor \nWednesday afternoon through Wednesday night/Thursday morning. \nThis is a quick hitter of a system but looks to pack a punch as \nsome colder air arrives with it as H7 temps drop to -3 to -8C \nrange Wednesday evening. This will bring valley rain and \nmountain snow with elevations above 10,000 feet favored for 4 to\n8 inches across the I-70 and central mountain corridor \nincluding the Elk Mountains. Decided to issue a Winter Weather \nAdvisory for COZ010 above 10,000 feet from 2 pm MST Wednesday \nthrough 5 am MST Thursday as roadways and passes above 10,000 \nfeet are expected to become slick and slushy impacting travel \nfor the Wednesday evening and Thursday morning commute. Slow \ndown and be prepared for winter driving conditions if you are \nout as road conditions could change rapidly as this system moves\nthrough. \n\nThere is some uncertainty though for convective potential which\ncould lead to heavier rain/snow rates, increasing potential\nimpacts. Localized flash flooding is possible if stronger rain \nshowers/storms develop over saturated or flood-prone areas, and \nhazardous travel conditions could develop over areas that see \nconvective bursts of snow even outside of the current Winter\nWeather Advisory. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD THURSDAY AND BEYOND:\n\nBeyond Thursday morning, the forecast overall looks to trend\nmuch drier. A quick moving shortwave skirts the region across\nthe north on Friday which could bring some increased clouds and\nisolated showers over the Divide with little impact. A broad\nridge of high pressure moves in late this week into the coming\nweekend, with temperatures warming to above seasonable levels\nand providing a much needed break from the recent wet weather.",
